.Em [name:]key=value ]
.Oo
.Fl propquery
.Em propq ]
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This command has been deprecated.
The
.Xr openssl-pkeyutl 1
command should be used instead.
.Pp
This command can be used to sign, verify, encrypt and decrypt data using
the RSA algorithm.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -help
Print out a usage message.
.It Sy -in Em filename
This specifies the input filename to read data from or standard input if
this option is not specified.
.It Sy -passin Em arg
The passphrase used in the output file.
See see
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1 .
.It Sy -rev
Reverse the order of the input.
.It Sy -out Em filename
Specifies the output filename to write to or standard output by default.
.It Sy -inkey Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
The input key, by default it should be an RSA private key.
.It Sy -keyform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM Ns | Ns Sy P12 Ns | Ns Sy ENGINE
The key format; unspecified by default.
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -pubin
By default a private key is read from the key input.
With this option a public key is read instead.
If the input contains no public key but a private key, its public part
is used.
.It Sy -certin
The input is a certificate containing an RSA public key.
.It Sy -sign
Sign the input data and output the signed result.
This requires an RSA private key.
.It Sy -verify
Verify the input data and output the recovered data.
.It Sy -encrypt
Encrypt the input data using an RSA public key.
.It Sy -decrypt
Decrypt the input data using an RSA private key.
.It Sy -pkcs Ns , Sy -oaep Ns , Sy -x931 Ns , Sy -raw
The padding to use: PKCS#1 v1.5 (the default), PKCS#1 OAEP, ANSI X9.31,
or no padding, respectively.
For signatures, only
.Sy -pkcs
and
.Sy -raw
can be used.
.Pp
Note: because of protection against Bleichenbacher attacks, decryption
using PKCS#1 v1.5 mode will not return errors in case padding check
failed.
Use
.Sy -raw
and inspect the returned value manually to check if the padding is
correct.
.It Sy -hexdump
Hex dump the output data.
.It Sy -asn1parse
Parse the ASN.1 output data, this is useful when combined with the
.Sy -verify
option.
.It Sy -engine Em id
See
.Xr openssl 1 .
This option is deprecated.
.It Sy -rand Em files Ns , Sy -writerand Em file
See
.Xr openssl 1
for details.
.It Sy -provider Em name
.It Sy -provider-path Em path
.It Sy -provparam Em [name:]key=value
.It Sy -propquery Em propq
See
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr provider 7 ,
and
.Xr property 7 .
.El
.Sh NOTES
Since this command uses the RSA algorithm directly, it can only be used
to sign or verify small pieces of data.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Examples equivalent to these can be found in the documentation for the
non-deprecated
.Xr openssl-pkeyutl 1
command.
.Pp
Sign some data using a private key:
.Bd -literal
 openssl rsautl -sign -in file -inkey key.pem -out sig
.Ed
.Pp
Recover the signed data
.Bd -literal
 openssl rsautl -verify -in sig -inkey key.pem
.Ed
.Pp
Examine the raw signed data:
.Bd -literal
 openssl rsautl -verify -in sig -inkey key.pem -raw -hexdump

 0000 - 00 01 ff ff ff ff ff ff-ff ff ff ff ff ff ff ff   ................
 0010 - ff ff ff ff ff ff ff ff-ff ff ff ff ff ff ff ff   ................
 0020 - ff ff ff ff ff ff ff ff-ff ff ff ff ff ff ff ff   ................
 0030 - ff ff ff ff ff ff ff ff-ff ff ff ff ff ff ff ff   ................
 0040 - ff ff ff ff ff ff ff ff-ff ff ff ff ff ff ff ff   ................
 0050 - ff ff ff ff ff ff ff ff-ff ff ff ff ff ff ff ff   ................
 0060 - ff ff ff ff ff ff ff ff-ff ff ff ff ff ff ff ff   ................
 0070 - ff ff ff ff 00 68 65 6c-6c 6f 20 77 6f 72 6c 64   .....hello world
.Ed
.Pp
The PKCS#1 block formatting is evident from this.
If this was done using encrypt and decrypt the block would have been of
type 2 (the second byte) and random padding data visible instead of the
0xff bytes.
.Pp
It is possible to analyse the signature of certificates using this
command in conjunction with
.Xr openssl-asn1parse 1 .
Consider the self signed example in
.Pa certs/pca-cert.pem .
Running
.Xr openssl-asn1parse 1
as follows yields:
.Bd -literal
 openssl asn1parse -in pca-cert.pem

    0:d=0  hl=4 l= 742 cons: SEQUENCE
    4:d=1  hl=4 l= 591 cons:  SEQUENCE
    8:d=2  hl=2 l=   3 cons:   cont [ 0 ]
   10:d=3  hl=2 l=   1 prim:    INTEGER           :02
   13:d=2  hl=2 l=   1 prim:   INTEGER           :00
   16:d=2  hl=2 l=  13 cons:   SEQUENCE
   18:d=3  hl=2 l=   9 prim:    OBJECT            :md5WithRSAEncryption
   29:d=3  hl=2 l=   0 prim:    NULL
   31:d=2  hl=2 l=  92 cons:   SEQUENCE
   33:d=3  hl=2 l=  11 cons:    SET
   35:d=4  hl=2 l=   9 cons:     SEQUENCE
   37:d=5  hl=2 l=   3 prim:      OBJECT            :countryName
   42:d=5  hl=2 l=   2 prim:      PRINTABLESTRING   :AU
  ....
  599:d=1  hl=2 l=  13 cons:  SEQUENCE
  601:d=2  hl=2 l=   9 prim:   OBJECT            :md5WithRSAEncryption
  612:d=2  hl=2 l=   0 prim:   NULL
  614:d=1  hl=3 l= 129 prim:  BIT STRING
.Ed
.Pp
The final BIT STRING contains the actual signature.
It can be extracted with:
.Bd -literal
 openssl asn1parse -in pca-cert.pem -out sig -noout -strparse 614
.Ed
.Pp
The certificate public key can be extracted with:
.Bd -literal
 openssl x509 -in test/testx509.pem -pubkey -noout >pubkey.pem
.Ed
.Pp
The signature can be analysed with:
.Bd -literal
 openssl rsautl -in sig -verify -asn1parse -inkey pubkey.pem -pubin

    0:d=0  hl=2 l=  32 cons: SEQUENCE
    2:d=1  hl=2 l=  12 cons:  SEQUENCE
    4:d=2  hl=2 l=   8 prim:   OBJECT            :md5
   14:d=2  hl=2 l=   0 prim:   NULL
   16:d=1  hl=2 l=  16 prim:  OCTET STRING
      0000 - f3 46 9e aa 1a 4a 73 c9-37 ea 93 00 48 25 08 b5   .F...Js.7...H%..
.Ed
.Pp
This is the parsed version of an ASN1 DigestInfo structure.
It can be seen that the digest used was md5. The actual part of the
certificate that was signed can be extracted with:
.Bd -literal
 openssl asn1parse -in pca-cert.pem -out tbs -noout -strparse 4
.Ed
.Pp
and its digest computed with:
.Bd -literal
 openssl md5 -c tbs
 MD5(tbs)= f3:46:9e:aa:1a:4a:73:c9:37:ea:93:00:48:25:08:b5
.Ed
.Pp
which it can be seen agrees with the recovered value above.
